  {
    track: 'Corporate knowledge manager / information specialist (BFSI, consulting, pharma, law)',
    pay: 'Roughly Rs 6-16+ LPA depending on employer size, seniority, and how much of the role covers information governance and systems work versus pure library operations',
  },
  {
    track: 'Independent information/taxonomy consultant or digital-archiving contractor',
    pay: 'Highly variable and project-based; not a fixed band, since it depends on client base, niche, and track record',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Assuming "library science" only leads to a school or government librarian post',
    body: 'Corporate knowledge management, digital archiving, and information-governance work are real, often better-paying directions for the exact same MLIS degree, and none of them require sitting behind a physical library desk all day.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Skipping UGC-NET because it "sounds like an academic-only exam"',
    body: 'It is the standard qualifying gate for most university, college, and many government library posts specifically, the segment of this field with the clearest, most stable pay ladder. Skipping it closes off that whole track early.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Treating every stage of government library recruitment as one exam',
    body: 'Clearing UGC-NET does not by itself get you a specific job. Most posts still run their own separate written exam, skill test, and interview after that, and later promotions run through their own selection process again. Plan for all of these stages, not just the first one.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Building zero digital or data skill alongside the library-science coursework',
    body: 'Cataloguing software, metadata standards, and basic database or spreadsheet fluency are now a real differentiator between a stagnant library-assistant role and a corporate knowledge-management or digital-archiving role that pays meaningfully more.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What are the career opportunities in library and information science?',
    a: 'They go well past a traditional librarian desk. Real tracks include school and public librarian roles, university and government librarian posts (via MLIS and UGC-NET), corporate knowledge management at law firms, consulting firms, BFSI, and pharma companies, digital archiving and digital-repository work, and, for experienced professionals, independent information or taxonomy consulting.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a good career after library science in India?',
    a: 'The honest answer depends on what you want. For stability and a clear pay ladder, a university or government librarian post via an MLIS and UGC-NET is the strongest structured route. For the highest realistic pay ceiling, corporate knowledge management and digital-archiving roles at large private companies and institutions currently pay more and scale faster with seniority than most government library posts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is UGC-NET compulsory for a career in library science?',
    a: 'Only for specific posts: Assistant Professor in library science, and, at most universities, university and college librarian posts. School librarian roles, library-assistant posts, and most corporate knowledge-management and digital-archiving roles typically hire on the BLIS/MLIS degree and relevant experience without requiring UGC-NET.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does clearing UGC-NET guarantee a librarian job?',
    a: 'No. UGC-NET qualifies you to apply for the post; it does not itself place you in one. Most university and government library posts still run their own separate recruitment process, a written exam, a skill test, and an interview, after UGC-NET, and this is a genuinely competitive, multi-stage process that can take more than one attempt.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What pays more: a government library job or corporate knowledge management?',
    a: 'A government or university librarian post follows a fixed pay-matrix scale that is predictable but capped by that scale. Corporate knowledge-management and digital-archiving roles at large private companies typically start around a similar or slightly higher figure but carry a meaningfully higher senior-level ceiling, especially at consulting firms, BFSI companies, and pharma R&D employers.',
  },
